1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,920
Rhinitis medicamentosa — if you've been reaching for that decongestant spray every single day for over a week, your nose isn't failing you.

2
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,240
The drug is rewiring how your blood vessels respond, and there's a real way out.

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:25,840
Oxymetazoline and similar sprays force nasal blood vessels to constrict on contact, but keep that up past three to five days and the vessels stop responding the same way — swelling snaps back harder the moment the drug wears off.

4
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:32,160
That rebound is exactly why so many people feel like they physically can't function without the bottle in their bag.

5
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:35,680
Quitting outright is the hardest part most people underestimate.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:45,840
Congestion typically spikes for the first several days after the last spray, and that spike alone sends most people reaching for it again before their nose gets the chance to reset.

7
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,640
A steroid nasal spray changes that math.

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:01:00,400
It works on inflammation directly instead of squeezing blood vessels, so it doesn't cause rebound even with daily use — starting one before your last dose of decongestant cushions the rough patch considerably.

9
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:08,080
If steroid sprays and tapering still aren't cutting it after weeks of trying, in-office procedures exist specifically for this.

10
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:15,280
One published study found the large majority of long-term users were able to stop for good after a single outpatient visit.

11
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:27,040
Talk to your doctor about a steroid spray first, brace for a rough few days once you stop the decongestant, and don't assume you're stuck if tapering alone doesn't work — there are more options than most people realize.
